        "items": [
          {
            "type": 1,
            "content": {
              "json": "## About the data in this workbook\r\n\r\nThis workbook mixes two data sources on purpose, because neither one alone can\r\ncover an Azure AI estate.\r\n\r\n### What Azure Resource Graph can and cannot see\r\n\r\nMeasured against live fixtures, not assumed from ARM template documentation:\r\n\r\n| Resource type | Indexed in Resource Graph? |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| `microsoft.cognitiveservices/accounts` | **Yes** - fully, and verified to match the ARM control plane exactly |\r\n| `microsoft.cognitiveservices/accounts/projects` (Foundry projects) | **Yes** |\r\n| `microsoft.machinelearningservices/workspaces` (Hub / Project / ML) | **Yes** - rich properties |\r\n| `microsoft.cognitiveservices/accounts/deployments` (**model deployments**) | **No - zero rows. Not indexed at all.** |\r\n\r\nFor accounts, each of `identity.type`, `disableLocalAuth`, `publicNetworkAccess`,\r\n`restrictOutboundNetworkAccess`, `networkAcls.defaultAction`, `networkAcls.ipRules`,\r\n`allowProjectManagement` and `customSubDomainName` was **set through ARM and then\r\nre-read in Resource Graph** to confirm it round-trips. A blank value therefore means\r\n*not configured*, never *not indexed*.\r\n\r\n### How model data is retrieved\r\n\r\nBecause model deployments are invisible to Resource Graph:\r\n\r\n1. A Resource Graph query lists every in-scope Azure OpenAI / Foundry account.\r\n2. The Model deployments tab reads the selected account's ARM `/deployments`\r\n   endpoint directly, with one grid row per returned deployment.\r\n3. The Model retirement tab reads the regional model catalog\r\n   (`.../locations/{region}/models`) for live retirement dates and lifecycle status.\r\n\r\nThe deployments grid is deliberately not merged with the catalog: Workbooks exposes\r\nonly one join-column pair, and a composite JSONPath key can evaluate empty in some\r\nportal runtimes, turning the merge into a deployment-by-catalog cross join.\r\nRetirement dates are never hard-coded - they come from the catalog at query time.\r\n\r\n### Findings\r\n\r\n**Accounts** - API key auth enabled instead of Entra ID only; public network access\r\nwith no private endpoint; network ACLs allowing all traffic; no managed identity;\r\nno custom subdomain (which blocks Entra ID auth); Microsoft-managed keys instead of\r\nCMK; unrestricted outbound access; failed provisioning.\r\n\r\n**Workspaces** - public access with no private link; managed VNet isolation disabled;\r\ndatastores using access keys instead of identity; no CMK; no managed identity; soft\r\ndelete off; v1 legacy mode; project not linked to a hub; failed provisioning.\r\n\r\n| Severity | Condition |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| Critical | 6 or more findings |\r\n| High | 4-5 findings |\r\n| Medium | 2-3 findings |\r\n| Low | 1 finding |\r\n| Healthy | No findings |\r\n\r\n### Companion script\r\n\r\n`Get-AIModelRetirement.ps1` sweeps every subscription, joins each deployment to the\r\nlive model catalog, and produces a computed report with **days remaining** and\r\nseverity - including flagging deployments set to `NoAutoUpgrade` that sit on a\r\nretiring version. That computation cannot be done inside a workbook because\r\nResource Graph cannot see deployments and ARM queries cannot post-process with KQL."
            },
            "name": "abHead"
